# Coldwater A.M. — the morning show on KSAB-TV.
#
# @type morning: a LINE LIBRARY, not a script. Every airing the server assembles a fresh
# episode from these pools and fills the {tokens} from the live world — the clock, the
# thermometer, the 7-day forecast, the news generator, the city's standing alerts. Bijou
# and Hal are real NPCs on the studio couch, so the show presence-gates like any live one.
#
# Every line is an EXCHANGE PAIR — "Bijou's line >> Hal's line". The setup and the deadpan
# always travel together, so whichever alternative gets drawn, the couch still works.

@broadcast "Coldwater A.M."
@category morning
@length 240
@type morning
@host npc_am_pace
@cohost npc_am_dorn
@titlecard coldwater_am_logo

# The compiler switches an ::asset to type 'svg' on seeing a leading <svg, so this block
# needs no directive of its own. The window frame is the joke: the sunrise behind it is
# banded like a screen because it IS one, and the corner admits as much.
::asset coldwater_am_logo
<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 640 360">
  <defs>
    <linearGradient id="cam-sky" x1="0" y1="0" x2="0" y2="1">
      <stop offset="0" stop-color="#2a1f45"/>
      <stop offset="0.45" stop-color="#8a3f6a"/>
      <stop offset="0.75" stop-color="#e8735a"/>
      <stop offset="1" stop-color="#ffc48a"/>
    </linearGradient>
    <linearGradient id="cam-sea" x1="0" y1="0" x2="0" y2="1">
      <stop offset="0" stop-color="#3a2d55"/>
      <stop offset="1" stop-color="#14112a"/>
    </linearGradient>
    <radialGradient id="cam-sun" cx="0.5" cy="0.5" r="0.5">
      <stop offset="0" stop-color="#fff2c4"/>
      <stop offset="0.6" stop-color="#ffd27a"/>
      <stop offset="1" stop-color="#ff9a5a"/>
    </radialGradient>
  </defs>
  <rect width="640" height="360" fill="url(#cam-sky)"/>
  <circle cx="320" cy="262" r="150" fill="#ffb27a" opacity="0.18"/>
  <circle cx="320" cy="262" r="104" fill="url(#cam-sun)"/>
  <g fill="#e8735a" opacity="0.55">
    <rect x="200" y="214" width="240" height="4"/>
    <rect x="200" y="230" width="240" height="5"/>
    <rect x="200" y="246" width="240" height="6"/>
  </g>
  <rect x="0" y="262" width="640" height="98" fill="url(#cam-sea)"/>
  <g fill="#ffd27a" opacity="0.7">
    <rect x="262" y="272" width="116" height="3" rx="1.5"/>
    <rect x="280" y="286" width="80" height="3" rx="1.5"/>
    <rect x="296" y="300" width="48" height="3" rx="1.5"/>
    <rect x="308" y="314" width="24" height="3" rx="1.5"/>
  </g>
  <g fill="#1d1733">
    <rect x="18" y="226" width="30" height="36"/>
    <rect x="56" y="206" width="24" height="56"/>
    <rect x="64" y="186" width="6" height="22"/>
    <rect x="88" y="236" width="40" height="26"/>
    <rect x="136" y="218" width="22" height="44"/>
    <rect x="482" y="222" width="34" height="40"/>
    <rect x="524" y="198" width="24" height="64"/>
    <rect x="532" y="178" width="6" height="22"/>
    <rect x="556" y="232" width="36" height="30"/>
    <rect x="600" y="214" width="26" height="48"/>
  </g>
  <g text-anchor="middle">
    <text x="320" y="120" fill="#fff4ea" font-family="Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif" font-size="68" font-style="italic" letter-spacing="1">Coldwater</text>
    <rect x="262" y="136" width="116" height="40" rx="20" fill="#ff6f91"/>
    <text x="320" y="165" fill="#fff4ea" font-family="Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif" font-size="26" font-weight="bold" letter-spacing="8">A.M.</text>
    <text x="320" y="344" fill="#ffd9c2" font-family="Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif" font-size="16" font-style="italic">&#8220;You&#8217;re up. Might as well be.&#8221;</text>
  </g>
  <g font-family="Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif">
    <rect x="28" y="28" width="72" height="22" rx="11" fill="#fff4ea"/>
    <text x="64" y="44" text-anchor="middle" fill="#8a3f6a" font-size="13" font-weight="bold" letter-spacing="2">KSAB-7</text>
    <text x="612" y="44" text-anchor="end" fill="#ffd9c2" font-size="10" letter-spacing="1" opacity="0.7">SUNRISE: COMPOSITE</text>
  </g>
</svg>
::endasset

::asset am_basin_beat_card

▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄
█  ☀  THE  BASIN  BEAT  ☀       █
█   stories from your street    █
▀▀▀▀▀▀▀▀▀▀▀▀▀▀▀▀▀▀▀▀▀▀▀▀▀▀▀▀▀▀▀▀▀

::endasset

# ── Cold open — reads the real clock, the real thermometer, the real weekday ──
::lines open
Good MORNING, Coldwater! It is {time}, it is {temp} degrees, and you are ALIVE! >> Statistically.
Good morning, Coldwater. {day}, {time}, {temp} degrees. >> All four of those are true and only one of them is good news.
It is {time} on a {season} {day} and you have made it to another one! >> Made it to. Not through.
{time}. {temp} degrees. I'm Bijou Pace. >> Hal Dorn.
Rise and SHINE, Basin! {temp} degrees, feels like {feels}! >> It does not feel like {feels}. It feels like {feels} and a grudge.
::endlines

::lines couch
Hal, did you sleep? >> I closed my eyes at the appropriate hour and time passed.
We have SUCH a show for you this morning. >> We have a show. The adjective is doing the heavy lifting.
Behind us, as always, the sunrise. >> That is a loop. That sunrise happened once, in a year none of us can verify.
How are we feeling this morning, Hal? >> Present.
::endlines

# ── Weather window — keyed to what the sky is ACTUALLY doing ──────────────────
::lines weather.banner
THE WINDOW | WHAT IT IS DOING TO YOU OUT THERE
::endlines

::lines weather
Out the window: {weather}, {temp} degrees, feels like {feels}. >> Feels like {feels}. It always feels like {feels}.
Conditions right now: {weather}, {temp} degrees, wind {wind}. >> Dress for {feels}, not for {temp}. The thermometer is an optimist.
{weather} out there this morning, {temp} degrees, {precip} percent chance of getting wet. >> Round that up.
::endlines

::lines weather.clear
CLEAR skies, Coldwater! {temp} degrees and you can see all the way to the ridge! >> You can see all the way to the ridge. Take a moment with that.
Not a cloud, {temp} degrees. It's a beautiful morning. >> It's a visible morning. Beautiful is a stretch.
::endlines

::lines weather.rain
Rain, {temp} degrees, {precip} percent and climbing. >> It is raining on the window that is not a window. There is something in that.
Wet one out there. Rain, feels like {feels}. >> Everything you own will be damp by ten and stay damp until spring.
::endlines

::lines weather.snow
Snow this morning! {temp} degrees. >> Snow is rain that decided to take its time about ruining your day.
It's snowing, it's {temp}, and it is very pretty from in here. >> From in here.
::endlines

::lines weather.fog
Fog on the Basin, {temp} degrees, can't see past the Locks. >> The fog is doing us a favour. Let it.
Thick fog this morning. Take it slow. >> Take it slow, or arrive somewhere unplanned and permanent.
::endlines

::lines weather.ash
Ashfall, Coldwater. {temp} degrees. Cover your mouth out there. >> Do not look up with your mouth open. I shouldn't have to say it annually.
Ash coming down again, {temp} degrees, wind {wind}. >> Shake your coat out before you bring it inside. That's it. That's the segment.
::endlines

::lines weather.thunderstorm
Thunderstorms, {temp} degrees, wind at {wind}. >> If you can hear it, it can reach you.
Storms over the Basin this morning. >> The Basin has never won one of these.
::endlines

::lines weather.severe
The Service has this at a WARNING level, Hal. >> Then stay in. Whatever it is you were going to do, it will keep.
This one has teeth. Wind {wind}, feels like {feels}. >> If you have somewhere with walls, be in it. If you don't, find someone who does.
Severe conditions today, Coldwater. >> Severe means the city stops pretending it can protect you. Act accordingly.
::endlines

# Hero-event mornings. The forecast has been carrying these for a week, so the
# couch isn't breaking news — it's the last reminder before it lands.
::lines weather.acid
It's an acid day, Hal. >> Then it's a stay-in day. I mean it. It goes through a coat like the coat isn't there.
The Service has the acid warning up. >> If you own sealed rainwear, today is what you bought it for. If you don't, today is what you should have.
Caustic rain by this afternoon. >> And to everyone about to tell me they'll "risk it": the wards fill up every single time.
::endlines

::lines weather.ion
Ion storm's due today. >> Grid goes down, screens go down, and anything in your pocket with a chip in it goes down with them.
Pulse warning, Coldwater. >> Leave the good deck at home. Whatever you're carrying, assume you're carrying it into a fire.
They're calling for a full ion event. >> If you're chromed, be sitting down when it hits. That's not a joke, that's the whole advice.
::endlines

::lines weather.ahead
Looking ahead: tomorrow it's {tomorrow}, around {tomorrowTemp}. >> Something to build towards.
Week's running a high of {hi} and a low of {lo}. >> The low is the one that counts.
Tomorrow: {tomorrow}. >> Same as today, then, but you'll be more tired.
::endlines

# ── The Basin Beat — the live news feed, read off the couch ───────────────────
::lines beat.banner
THE BASIN BEAT | STORIES FROM YOUR STREET
::endlines

::lines beat.lead
First up, {headline}! >> And there it is.
Our top story this morning: {headline}. >> Mm.
Here's one for you, Hal. {headline}. >> I don't want it.
{headline}! Isn't that something? >> It's something.
Now THIS is a heartwarming one, {headline}! >> That's not what heartwarming means.
::endlines

::lines beat.detail
{byline} has it that {body} >> {byline} would.
The details, and they are details. {body} >> They put that in writing. Someone typed that and let it leave the building.
Here's what we know: {body} >> Here's what we know. Note the shape of the parts they left out.
::endlines

::lines beat.aside
Moving on! >> We're not going to sit with that at all, are we.
And we'll stay on that one as it develops. >> It won't develop. It'll be replaced.
::endlines

# ── The rotating segment — the hotplate bit and its relatives ─────────────────
::lines segment.banner
THE HOTPLATE | THE MOST IMPORTANT MEAL YOU CAN AFFORD
::endlines

::lines segment
Now, breakfast! Take your standard ration paste, squeeze it into a pan, and here's the trick: heat. >> That's the trick.
Two minutes on the ring and the paste goes from grey to a sort of WARMER grey. >> My father used to do that.
Got a little sweetener? Stir it through at the end and you have got yourself a TREAT. >> You have got yourself a warmer grey.
Today's tip: crack the window while you cook. >> Today's tip: know which of your neighbours can smell that you ate.
Letter from a viewer in Meltwater Row. She wants to know if the sunrise is real. >> Next letter.
Cold morning like this, wear your layers LOOSE. Trapped air is warm air. >> Trapped air is warm air. Trapped people are not. Loose layers.
::endlines

# ── Your Morning Run-In — reads the city's actual standing alerts ─────────────
::lines runin.banner
YOUR MORNING RUN-IN | WHAT THE CITY IS DOING TO YOU TODAY
::endlines

::lines runin.clear
Curfew lifts on schedule, gates are moving, the Locks are open. >> Which means today, if something goes wrong, it'll be your fault.
Nothing standing on the boards this morning, a clean run-in. >> Enjoy it. Write it down.
Roads are roads, gates are gates, and the Basin is BEAUTIFUL this morning. >> If you don't look directly at it.
::endlines

::lines runin.blackout
Grid crews are out, {outages} blocks dark this morning. >> If your block is one of them, don't open the freezer and don't trust the stairwell.
We're hearing {outages} blocks off the grid. >> Charge what you can while you can. It comes back when it comes back.
::endlines

::lines runin.martial_law
Curfew is in effect BASIN-WIDE and it is being enforced. >> Enforced means they don't have to explain afterwards. Be somewhere with a door.
Patrols are up across every district this morning. >> Carry your papers. Carry nothing else.
::endlines

::lines runin.radiation
There's a radiation advisory standing this morning. >> Seal what you can, stay off the high ground, and don't drink anything that came from a puddle.
The counters are up across the Basin. >> Indoors. All day if you can manage it.
::endlines

::lines runin.storm
The weather's going to make a mess of the run-in, so allow extra. >> Allow extra or don't go. Those are the two options and only one of them is free.
Gates will be slow with this coming in. >> They always slow the gate when the sky turns. It's the one thing they're consistent about.
::endlines

::lines ticker.lead
COLDWATER A.M.
::endlines

# ── Sign-off ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
::lines signoff
That's us! Back tomorrow, same couch, same sunrise. >> Same sunrise.
Go and have a WONDERFUL day, Coldwater. You've earned it. >> Probably.
That's your morning. We'll be here. >> We're always here. That's not the same as being fine.
Coldwater A.M. is brought to you by the people who would like you at work on time. >> On time.
::endlines

::lines credits
COLDWATER A.M.
Presented by Bijou Pace and Hal Dorn.
Hal Dorn appears by arrangement with his contract.
The sunrise depicted is a composite and does not reflect current conditions.
::endlines

END
